WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.094
The drone now works for a delivery service

2
00:00:06.094 --> 00:00:08.546
five landing pads on a hillside

3
00:00:08.546 --> 00:00:10.180
joined by six routes.

4
00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.425
Each route has an arrow

5
00:00:12.425 --> 00:00:14.952
from a start pad to an end pad.

6
00:00:14.952 --> 00:00:17.241
The arrow only fixes a sign

7
00:00:17.241 --> 00:00:19.610
crates can travel either way.

8
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.060
The three arrows on the left

9
00:00:22.135 --> 00:00:23.485
make one triangle

10
00:00:23.485 --> 00:00:26.260
the three on the right make another

11
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.960
and they meet at landing pad three

12
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.160
like a bow tie.

13
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:32.823
A surveyor measures how much

14
00:00:32.905 --> 00:00:34.392
each route climbs.

15
00:00:34.392 --> 00:00:37.118
Without knowing a single height

16
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.678
how could you tell whether the

17
00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:41.826
six numbers fit together?

18
00:00:41.826 --> 00:00:43.230
Pause and think.

19
00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.721
One matrix records the whole network

20
00:00:49.721 --> 00:00:51.609
the incidence matrix A.

21
00:00:51.687 --> 00:00:54.283
It has one row for each route and

22
00:00:54.362 --> 00:00:56.879
one column for each landing pad

23
00:00:56.879 --> 00:00:58.610
so it is six by five.

24
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.131
Row one is route e one

25
00:01:01.131 --> 00:01:03.491
from pad one to pad two

26
00:01:03.491 --> 00:01:07.456
minus one in column one, where it starts

27
00:01:07.456 --> 00:01:11.138
plus one in column two, where it ends

28
00:01:11.138 --> 00:01:13.120
and zeros elsewhere.

29
00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.511
The other five rows follow the same rule

30
00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:21.750
e two, e three, e four, e five, and e six.

31
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.774
Each row has one minus one and one plus one

32
00:01:25.774 --> 00:01:28.029
so every row adds to zero.

33
00:01:28.112 --> 00:01:30.367
Rows are routes and columns

34
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.870
are landing pads

35
00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:35.460
swap them and you get A transpose instead.

36
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:40.904
Now give each pad a height in metres: ten

37
00:01:40.904 --> 00:01:45.652
fourteen, thirteen, twenty and eighteen.

38
00:01:45.652 --> 00:01:48.200
That is the vector x.

39
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.343
Each row of A times x gives the

40
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:54.225
height where a route ends minus

41
00:01:54.315 --> 00:01:56.748
the height where it starts

42
00:01:56.748 --> 00:01:59.270
the climb along that route.

43
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.612
Route e one climbs four

44
00:02:02.612 --> 00:02:05.354
from ten to fourteen.

45
00:02:05.354 --> 00:02:07.473
e two drops one.

46
00:02:07.473 --> 00:02:12.334
Then three, seven, minus two and five

47
00:02:12.334 --> 00:02:15.700
six climbs, one per route.

48
00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.981
Which heights give no climb at all?

49
00:02:19.058 --> 00:02:22.222
A x equals zero says that the two ends of

50
00:02:22.299 --> 00:02:25.000
every route are at the same height.

51
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.331
You can walk from any landing pad

52
00:02:28.421 --> 00:02:31.221
to any other along the network

53
00:02:31.221 --> 00:02:34.021
so all five heights are equal.

54
00:02:34.021 --> 00:02:37.995
The nullspace is the line through one, one

55
00:02:37.995 --> 00:02:39.350
one, one, one.

56
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.878
In words: raise every pad by seven metres

57
00:02:42.878 --> 00:02:45.300
and not a single climb changes.

58
00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.064
So the nullspace has dimension one

59
00:02:49.064 --> 00:02:52.771
and the rank is five minus one: four.

60
00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:55.502
Fix pad one at height zero

61
00:02:55.502 --> 00:02:59.891
and the heights become relative to it: zero

62
00:02:59.891 --> 00:03:02.330
four, three, ten, eight.

63
00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.729
Back to the surveyor

64
00:03:04.729 --> 00:03:08.627
which lists of climbs can come from heights?

65
00:03:08.627 --> 00:03:12.179
Walk around the left triangle: up e one

66
00:03:12.179 --> 00:03:15.384
along e two, and back along e three

67
00:03:15.384 --> 00:03:17.030
against its arrow.

68
00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.122
You come back to the height you started at

69
00:03:21.122 --> 00:03:24.864
so b one plus b two minus b three is zero

70
00:03:24.864 --> 00:03:27.300
four minus one minus three.

71
00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.100
The right triangle gives b four plus

72
00:03:31.196 --> 00:03:34.167
b five minus b six equals zero

73
00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:36.850
seven minus two minus five.

74
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.855
This is the loop law

75
00:03:38.855 --> 00:03:41.849
climbs add to zero around every loop.

76
00:03:41.928 --> 00:03:44.292
Each loop is a vector y with y

77
00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.419
transpose A equal to zero

78
00:03:46.419 --> 00:03:49.650
so the loops live in the left nullspace

79
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.880
they are the tests for the column space.

80
00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.346
It is tempting to think six

81
00:03:55.415 --> 00:03:57.859
climbs can be anything: six routes

82
00:03:57.859 --> 00:03:59.186
five free heights.

83
00:03:59.256 --> 00:04:01.072
But five heights give only

84
00:04:01.141 --> 00:04:02.817
four independent climbs.

85
00:04:02.887 --> 00:04:04.982
The column space has dimension

86
00:04:05.052 --> 00:04:06.309
four inside R six

87
00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:08.404
and the two loops are the two

88
00:04:08.474 --> 00:04:09.940
conditions left over.

89
00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:14.080
Now a surveyor reports the same climbs

90
00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.412
but six on route e six.

91
00:04:16.412 --> 00:04:19.717
The left loop still adds to zero.

92
00:04:19.814 --> 00:04:22.340
The right loop gives seven

93
00:04:22.438 --> 00:04:25.450
minus two minus six: minus one.

94
00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.660
So no heights at all fit this survey

95
00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.056
it is not in the column space

96
00:04:31.133 --> 00:04:33.142
and the error is somewhere

97
00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.920
on the right triangle.

98
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.238
So: the incidence matrix turns heights on

99
00:04:39.329 --> 00:04:42.520
the pads into climbs on the routes.

100
00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.437
Its nullspace is equal heights

101
00:04:45.437 --> 00:04:49.540
so its rank is the number of pads minus one.

102
00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:54.347
And a list of climbs comes from heights exactly

103
00:04:54.442 --> 00:04:58.141
when it adds to zero around every loop.

104
00:04:58.141 --> 00:05:02.124
Your turn: do the climbs one, zero, zero

105
00:05:02.124 --> 00:05:06.012
zero, zero, minus one come from heights?

106
00:05:06.012 --> 00:05:10.090
In part two, crates flow along the routes.
